The issue is that `FixBoolLogicalOps` prematurely assumes that if an operand (`LHS` or `RHS`) is the condition of the original `select` (`isBoolLogicOp`), it is safe from causing poison-related miscompilations. This assumption is incorrect because even if it was the condition of the original `select`, using it as the condition of a *new* `select` created during vectorization can introduce poison if that condition is itself poison and the transformation relies on it being defined. The correct criterion for skipping the `freeze` operation is whether the value is `isGuaranteedNotToBePoison`.

The fix removes the incorrect checks that rely solely on the operand being the condition of the original `select` instruction, ensuring that we only skip freezing if the value is known to be safe or if it is the reduction root.

